# Audio Render Example

**Category:** MIDI

**Tags:** Best Practice, Complex, MIDI Player

This snippet demonstrates how to use the `Engine.renderAudio` callback in order to create an audio file from a MIDI sequence (just as if you would bounce the audio in the DAW).

```javascript
/** Audio render example

Written by Bill Evans (and slightly refactored by Christoph Hart)

This snippet demonstrates how to use the Engine.renderAudio() callback in order to bounce 
the sequence of a single MIDI player to a audio file.

This is a rather simplified use case and you probably need to implement a more complicated 
system if you have multiple MIDI players which send their message to different child sound 
generators (in that case it's recommended to hook them up to a global transport handler and 
then just send a start and a stop message that will control all MIDI players at once)
*/

Content.makeFrontInterface(510, 160);

// Set this to true if you want to delete the temp file. If you're using it in a real project
// this might be a bit cleaner, since most DAWs will create a copy of the file (or if you want 
// you can make a better file handling system than using a single file in the temp folder, but 
// for this example we'll set it to false so that you can just drag it over to the audio waveform.
const var DELETE_TEMP_FILE = false;

// Get backing panel references.
const var SeqView = Content.addPanel("SeqView", 0, 90);
SeqView.set("width", 210);

// Get drag panel references.
const var AudioDragger = Content.addPanel("Dragger", 0, 32);
AudioDragger.set("width", 210);

AudioDragger.set("allowCallbacks", "Clicks & Hover");

// Add the MIDI player.
const var MidiPlayer = Synth.getMidiPlayer("MIDI Player1");

// Usually we can use the embedded assets, however since you might want
// to test exporting this as plugin we'll rather create the sequence programmatically...
MidiPlayer.create(4, 4, 1);
var eventList = [];

/** Adds a note to the event list. */
inline function addNote(eventListToUse, noteNumber, startQuarter, durationQuarter)
{
	local start_ts = MidiPlayer.getTicksPerQuarter() * startQuarter;
	local end_ts = start_ts + MidiPlayer.getTicksPerQuarter() * durationQuarter;

	local on = Engine.createMessageHolder();
	on.setType(1);			   // note on
	on.setNoteNumber(noteNumber);  // note number
	on.setChannel(1);		   // first channel
	on.setVelocity(127);	   // full velocity	
	on.setTimestamp(start_ts); // timestamp at note start

	eventListToUse.push(on);

	local off = Engine.createMessageHolder();
	off.setType(2);			   // note on
	off.setNoteNumber(noteNumber);  // note number
	off.setChannel(1);		   // first channel
	off.setTimestamp(end_ts); // timestamp at note start

	eventListToUse.push(off);
}

// C-Minor 7
const var CHORD = [48, 51, 55, 58];


// Push the notes to the event list.
for(i = 0; i < 4; i++)
{
	addNote(eventList, CHORD[i], i, 0.9);
}

// Now that is slightly annoying: if you export this as a plugin, the sample rate 
// is not properly initialised when this script runs. There are many ways to fix
// this issue, but the easiest might be to just swap to the MIDI tick domain for the
// MIDI creation
MidiPlayer.setUseTimestampInTicks(true);

MidiPlayer.flushMessageList(eventList);

// And now swap back so that the `getEventList()` call returns the timestamps in the
// required samples domain for the audio export.
MidiPlayer.setUseTimestampInTicks(false);

AudioDragger.setMouseCallback(function(event) 
{
	this.data.hover = event.hover;
	this.repaint();

	// Check whether the user has clicked on Render yet
	// (In a real project this would be handled better using the `enabled` property)
	if (event.clicked) 
	{ 
		if(isDefined(this.data.tempFile))
		{
			// The external file drag callback will be executed asynchronously 
			// (after the user has dropped) the file, so in order to delete the temp file 
			// we need to use the lambda concept to pass local variables into callbacks.
			var fToDelete = this.data.tempFile;

			// Enable the dragging UI system. Set true to copy the file because the DAW will need access.
			this.startExternalFileDrag(this.data.tempFile, true, function [fToDelete]()
			{
				if(DELETE_TEMP_FILE)
				{
					Console.print("DELETE " + fToDelete.toString(0));
					fToDelete.deleteFileOrDirectory();
				}
			});
		}
		else
		{
			// HISE Protip 2000: Prepend a ! before your console statement to make it appear as error...
			Console.print("! You need to click on render first.");
		}
	}
});

// ============================================= Button to Begin Rendering

const var Button1 = Content.addButton("Button1", 0, 0);
Button1.set("text", "Render");
Button1.set("isMomentary", true);
Button1.set("saveInPreset", false);
Button1.set("width", 210);

Button1.setControlCallback(onButton1Control);
inline function onButton1Control(component, value) {

	// If the button is pressed…
	if (value) {

		// If a sequencer is loaded…
		if (MidiPlayer.isEmpty() != true) {

			// grab the event list from the MIDI player
			local eventList = MidiPlayer.getEventList();

			// The exporter exports up the last event, so if the last note of the 
			// sequence is not exactly at the sequence end position, we'll end up with
			// a truncated loop. In order to fix this, we must manually inject a (random)
			// MIDI message at the very end of the sequence.

			// fetch the timesignature object to calculate the end of the track
			local timeSig = MidiPlayer.getTimeSignature();
			local length = timeSig.NumBars;
			length *= timeSig.Nominator;
			length *= (timeSig.Denominator / 4.0);
			length *= timeSig.LoopEnd;

			// Note: you don't need to use the MIDI tempo information from the timeSig
			// object as we're using the global BPM tempo for rendering.
			local ts = Engine.getSamplesForQuarterBeats(length);

			local stopEvent = Engine.createMessageHolder();
			stopEvent.setType(3);             // CC
			stopEvent.setChannel(1);          // first channel
			stopEvent.setControllerNumber(1); // Modwheel
			stopEvent.setControllerValue(0);  // all the way down
			stopEvent.setTimestamp(ts);       // at the end of the track

			eventList.push(stopEvent);

			// Render the audio.
			Engine.renderAudio(eventList, COMPOSER_RenderAudioCallback);	
		}
	}
};

// =================================================== Rendering Function

// This function is called by HISE every 500 ms or so, so you can provide a progress bar. 
inline function COMPOSER_RenderAudioCallback (obj) {

		// Grab these variables from the JSON object.
		local isFinished = obj.finished;		
		local progressPoint = obj.progress;

		if (isFinished) {

			// Store the buffer in a Panel's data slot. Depending on whether the buffer is mono or not,
			// we pass either a buffer reference, or a reference to a JSON object containing buffer references.
			// (It can also be considered an array of channels).
			AudioDragger.data.buffer = obj.channels;

			// Assign a temporary variable to hold the buffer. This is simply to increase readability.
			local bufferDataToWrite = AudioDragger.data.buffer;

			// Get a reference to the temp folder.
			local audioFilesDirectory = FileSystem.getFolder(FileSystem.Temp);

			// Create the new file and get a reference to it. This will overwrite a file of the same name,
			// so be careful.
			local fileToExport = audioFilesDirectory.getChildFile("export_temp.wav");

			// See if the file is mono or stereo. If 
			local isMultiChannel = isDefined(bufferDataToWrite[0].length);

			// Export the file. 
			if (!isMultiChannel)
				fileToExport.writeAudioFile([bufferDataToWrite], Engine.getSampleRate(), 24);
			else
				fileToExport.writeAudioFile(bufferDataToWrite, Engine.getSampleRate(), 24);

			// Store the temp file reference in the panel. When the panel is dragged, it will move the temp
			// file to the other location
			AudioDragger.data.tempFile = fileToExport;

			enableBroadcaster.sendAsyncMessage([true]);

			// Remove this line later but it will point you to the temp file that has been created.
			fileToExport.show();

		} else {

			// Progress is a float from 0 to 1.
			Console.print( "Progress: " + progressPoint);
		}
}

// This function creates a 2D graph of the sequence. I don't check to see if there is a sequence loaded, because
// that's how I clear the graph if no sequence is loaded. Maybe there's a more elegant way.
SeqView.setPaintRoutine(function(g) 
{
	g.fillAll(0x11FFFFFF);

	// Get the area of the enclosing panel.
	var displayArea = this.getLocalBounds(0);

	// Taking the data from the MIDI Player, create a list of 4-parameter arrays (start x, start y, width and height)
	// to specify the geometry and relative position of each note-rectangle.
	var noteRectangleList = MidiPlayer.getNoteRectangleList(displayArea);

	// Set the fill colour for each note-rectangle.
	g.setColour(Colours.white);

	// Iterate over each note-rectangle.
	for(noteRectangle in noteRectangleList) 
	{
		// Pass each array to the HISE API for drawing rectangles.
		g.fillRect(noteRectangle);
	}
});

// Broadcaster definition
const var enableBroadcaster = Engine.createBroadcaster({
  "id": "enableBroadcaster",
  "args": ["shouldBeEnabled"],
  "tags": []
});


// attach first listener
enableBroadcaster.addComponentPropertyListener(["Dragger"], ["enabled"], "set the enabled state for the dragger", 
function(index, shouldBeEnabled){
	return shouldBeEnabled;
});

enableBroadcaster.addComponentPropertyListener(["Dragger"], ["text"], "update the text", 
function(index, shouldBeEnabled){
	return shouldBeEnabled ? "Drag to waveform or external target" : "Press render";
});

enableBroadcaster.addComponentRefreshListener(["Dragger"], "repaint", "repaint");
enableBroadcaster.sendAsyncMessage([false]);


inline function drawDragPanel(g)
{
	g.fillAll(0x22FFFFFF);
	g.setColour(this.data.hover ? Colours.white : 0xBBFFFFFF);

	if(!this.get("enabled"))
		g.setColour(0x44FFFFFF);

	g.setFont("Default", 14.0);
	g.drawAlignedText(this.get("text"), this.getLocalBounds(0), "centred");
}

AudioDragger.setPaintRoutine(drawDragPanel);

