# Clocksynced Arpeggiator

**Category:** MIDI

**Tags:** Best Practice, Complex, Featured, Host Sync

A complete redesign of the arpeggiator module using the clock-synced transport handler. 

> [Forum discussion](https://forum.hise.audio/topic/9497/browsable-snippet-clocksynced-arpeggiator) 

For new projects I would recommend using this as a starting point and then adjust it to your liking instead of relying on me fixing the old arpeggiator.

```javascript
/** Clock synced Arpeggiator

This script implements an arpeggiator using the clock grid callback of the transport handler.

It tries to reuse most of the parameters of the stock HISE arpeggiator (except for exotic weird stuff
that shouldn't have made it in the original Arpeggiator in the first place, stride, MPE channel stuff, etc).

The big advantage of using the transport handler as the main driver for the arp is that it automatically syncs
to the DAW position, so that the timing is consistent and locked to the DAW grid and the pressed notes 
will only control which notes it will play.

If you don't want that behaviour (so that if playing a note mid-bar should reset the arpeggiator position, 
you can achieve this by using the `PreferInternal` sync mode. 

The only real limitation of this approach is that it doesn't allow multiple arpeggiators to run simultaneuosly 
(because there's only one grid callback) but that is a rather exotic use case

Other than that the code is much much (much) more readable than the original Arpeggiator and shoul
be customizable without too much mental overhead.

** ADDITION: CUSTOM EVENT DATA **

With the recent addition of a HISE-wide event storage object, the arpeggiator was extended to write additional data (up to
16 lanes) that can be picked up by the HISE EventData Modulator at any point in the signal chain.

Currently the first lane is assigned to the filter frequency and the second to the panning, but that is completely arbirtrary
and can even be assigned dynamically!

Just select one of the 16 lanes in the Addional Lane drop down, then edit the sequence and add more modulation targets whereever you like...
*/

// If you port this to your project, just copy the entire module over and  remove these lines 
// so that it won't mess with your actual interface...
Content.makeFrontInterface(830, 440);
Synth.deferCallbacks(false);

namespace SyncedArpeggiator
{

const var th = Engine.createTransportHandler();
th.setSyncMode(th.PreferExternal);
th.setEnableGrid(true, 8);

//! UI CONTROL DEFINITION
// ==========================================================================

const var X_OFFSET = 530;
const var Y_OFFSET = 70;

// The tempo knob that controls the speed of the grid callback.
const var tempo = Content.addKnob("Tempo", X_OFFSET + 0, Y_OFFSET);
tempo.set("mode", "TempoSync");

inline function onTempoControl(component, value)
{
	th.setEnableGrid(true, value);
};
tempo.setControlCallback(onTempoControl);

// The mode combobox that sets the play order of the arpeggiator
const var mode = Content.addComboBox("Mode", X_OFFSET + 0, Y_OFFSET + 130);
mode.set("items", ["Up", "Down", "Up / Down", "Down / Up", "Order", "Random", "Chord"].join("\n"));
mode.set("width", 90);

// Write this as constants so we can use a neat switch statement later.

const var MODE_UP = 1;
const var MODE_DOWN = 2;
const var MODE_UPDOWN = 3;
const var MODE_DOWNUP = 4;
const var MODE_ORDER = 5;
const var MODE_RANDOM = 6;
const var MODE_CHORD = 7;

// These state variables will be set by the mode combobox
// and decide how to traverse the note array
reg inverse = false;			// the direction to be used when starting the arp
reg currentInverse = inverse;   // the currently used direction
reg swapInverse = true;			// whether to swap the direction at the end of the note list
reg randomNote = false;			// whether to just randomly select a note within the note list
reg chordMode = false;			// whether to play all notes at once

reg firstChord = false;

inline function onModeControl(component, value)
{
	// This switch statement will set the state variables
	// obviously this could be optimized, but this should be the 
	// clearest syntax.
	switch(value)
	{
		case MODE_UP:
		    inverse = false;
		    swapInverse = false;
		    randomNote = false;
		    chordMode = false;
		    break;
		case MODE_DOWN:
		    inverse = true;
		    swapInverse = false;
		    randomNote = false;
		    chordMode = false;
		case MODE_UPDOWN:
		   	inverse = true;
		   	swapInverse = true;
		   	randomNote = false;
		   	chordMode = false;
		   	break;  
		case MODE_DOWNUP:
		   	inverse = false;
		   	swapInverse = true;
		   	randomNote = false;
		   	chordMode = false;
		   	break;
		case MODE_RANDOM:
			inverse = false;
		   	swapInverse = false;
		   	randomNote = true;
		   	chordMode = false;
		   	break;		
		case MODE_ORDER:
			inverse = false;
		    swapInverse = false;
		    randomNote = false;
		    chordMode = false;
		    break;
		case MODE_CHORD:
			chordMode = true;
			break;
	}

	// We can tell the fix object factory to use different properties for sorting
	// so we either pass in the note number as property to sort or the uptime.
	// Note: the ID will always be 
	f.setCompareFunction(value == MODE_ORDER ? "uptime,noteNumber" : "noteNumber,uptime");
	rebuildSortedList();
};

mode.setControlCallback(onModeControl);

// The number of octaves. This will create octaved copies of the currently held notes
const var numOctaves = Content.addKnob("Octaves", X_OFFSET + 0, Y_OFFSET + 60);
numOctaves.set("min", 1);
numOctaves.set("max", 5);
numOctaves.set("stepSize", 1);

inline function onOctavesControl(component, value)
{
	rebuildSortedList();
};

numOctaves.setControlCallback(onOctavesControl);

// The number of steps. This will resize the slider packs and cause the grid callback index
// to be wrapped around the upper limit.
const var NumSteps = Content.addKnob("NumSteps", X_OFFSET + 130, Y_OFFSET + 60);

inline function onNumStepsControl(component, value)
{
	VelocityPack.set("sliderAmount", value);
	LengthPack.set("sliderAmount", value);
	PitchPack.set("sliderAmount", value);

	for(ad in additionalData)
	{
		ad.setNumSliders(value);
	}
};

NumSteps.setControlCallback(onNumStepsControl);

// The swing. It will add a delay to every odd note to simulate shuffle baby
const var swing = Content.addKnob("Swing", X_OFFSET + 130, Y_OFFSET + 0);
swing.set("mode", "NormalizedPercentage");

// Bypass. Use this instead of the global module bypass so that it can clean up the internal state
// properly (otherwise you'll get hanging notes)
const var enabled = Content.addButton("Enabled", X_OFFSET + 95 + 75, Y_OFFSET + 130);

enabled.set("width", 88);
enabled.set("height", 32);

inline function onBypassControl(component, value)
{
	if(!value)
	{
		activeNotes.clearQuick();
		sortedList.clearQuick();
		th.stopInternalClock(0);
	}
	else
	{

		Engine.allNotesOff();
	}
};

