# Scriptnode Visualisation 101

**Category:** Scriptnode

**Tags:** Best Practice, ScriptPanel, UI Logic

This snippet shows the two most convenient options for displaying any signal from within a 
Scriptnode network onto your UI. 

There are of course many other options with increasing hackiness, 
but these two solutions should be preferred unless your specific project requires another solution.

```javascript
/** Scriptnode Visualisation 101


If you need a full 2D plot of the signal, use Option 1.

If you only need a one-dimensional value that shows the current value 
(for stuff like mod-rings around a knob or VU meters), I would suggest Option 2. 

The scriptnode network here is just a mod chain with a slow LFO that is fed 
into a peak node and a global_cable node.
*/

Content.makeFrontInterface(220, 50);

// Option 1: Use external display buffers
// ==============================================================================

// Get the script FX module as the display buffer source
const var dp = Synth.getDisplayBufferSource("Script FX1");

// Get the first external display buffer (in our case the peak node that we set 
// to be the External Display Buffer #1)
const var rb = dp.getDisplayBuffer(0);

const var BUFFER_LENGTH = 16384;

// These are the properties that you can give the peak meter
// NumChannels must be 1, but you can set another buffer length for the display
const var properties = {
  "BufferLength": BUFFER_LENGTH,
  "NumChannels": 1
};

// This will override the default properties of the node. You can use the same
// procedure to customize the FFT properties too (like Window type, etc).
rb.setRingBufferProperties(properties);

// Now let's add a simple panel for displaying the signal
const var P1 = Content.addPanel("P1", 0, 0);

// We need to periodically query the ring buffer to create a signal path
// so we use the panel's timers for this.
P1.setTimerCallback(function()
{
	// The autocomplete fails somehow with supplying you the methods,
	// but you can look in the API Browser under ScriptRingBuffer
	// This method creates a path object to mirror the peak display.
	this.data.buffer = rb.createPath(this.getLocalBounds(0), // area
									 [0, BUFFER_LENGTH, 0, 1.0], // [x_start, x_end, y_min, y_max]
									 0.0 // start value (the initial value at position 0)
									 );
	this.repaint();
});

P1.startTimer(30);

P1.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
	g.fillAll(0x22FFFFFF);
	g.setColour(Colours.white);
	g.fillPath(this.data.buffer, this.getLocalBounds(0));
});


// Option 2: Use global cables
// ================================================================================

// Create a reference to the global routing manager
const var rm = Engine.getGlobalRoutingManager();

// Create a reference to the global cable we want to visualize
const var mc = rm.getCable("mod_output");

// Add another panel. This will just display a circle with the alpha value modulated
// by the cable output. You can use this as starting point for VU meters, or LED thingies...
const var P2 = Content.addPanel("P2", 110, 0);

// Now instead of the timer we use the asynchronous UI callback provided by the cable.
mc.registerCallback(function(value)
{
	// Write the value into the data object and repaint the panel
	P2.data.value = value;
	P2.repaint();
}, AsyncNotification);

P2.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
	g.fillAll(0x22FFFFFF);
	g.setColour(Colours.withAlpha(Colours.white, this.data.value));
	// Rawdogging the area like a pro...
	g.fillEllipse([25, 0, 50, 50]);	
});


