# Exclusive Matrix Modulation Source

**Category:** UI

**Tags:** Simple, Modulation

This snippet demonstrates how to use the setSourceSelectionCallback() to limit the mod draggers 
that are displayed when you hover over a knob.

It expands on the concept of the Matrix Modulation Tutorial so if you want to start learning about that system, this would be a better starting point.

```javascript
/** Matrix Modulator Tutorial Pt. II

Exclusive Source Selection UI Logic
===================================

This snippet demonstrates how to use the setSourceSelectionCallback() to limit the mod draggers
that are displayed when you hover over a knob. 

1. Drag a few connections on the two knobs (you'll see which connections are established on the right side). On compilation it will reset all connections for demonstration purposes.
2. You'll see that whatever dragger you click on, it will be highlighted (like a button with a radio group). This indicates the "current" source selection.
3. If you now hover over the UI knobs, it will only show a single mod knob (if the current source is connected to the knob). Take a look at the script below how this is implemented.

*/
Content.makeFrontInterface(600, 400);

/** Create a modulation matrix. */
const var mm = Engine.createModulationMatrix("Global Modulator Container1");

/** Reset all connections on compilation. */
mm.clearAllConnections("");

var CURRENTLY_SELECTED_SOURCE = "";

/** Attach a callback to the source selection event and just store the current source. */
mm.setSourceSelectionCallback(function(source)
{
	CURRENTLY_SELECTED_SOURCE = source;
});

const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

/** Now we override the method that will create the position data. 

We'll leave the default rendering function in there so it will look ass, but this snippet just wants to
demonstrate how to use that method for custom hover mod layouts. 
*/
function singleDragger(obj)
{
	/** Create the target area for whatever hover dragger we want to show. */
	var area = Rectangle(obj.sliderBounds[0], obj.sliderBounds[1] + obj.sliderBounds[3], 100, 120);

	/** Now check all connections and only display the active one. */
	for(c in obj.connections)
	{
		if(c == CURRENTLY_SELECTED_SOURCE)
		{
			/** Leave a little at the bottom for the value label... */
			obj.dragAreas.push(area.removeFromTop(100));
		}
		else
		{
			/** Now this is a bit hacky, but you cannot pass in an empty rectangle.
				So what's the second best option? Yes, a rectangle with 1px x 1px size.

				Note that it also creates a total rectangle from all positions that you pass in here
				in order to figure out when to dismiss the hover popup, so if you pass in the 
				rectangle with the origin, the hover behaviour gets weird.
			*/
			obj.dragAreas.push(Rectangle(area[0], area[1], 1, 1));
		}		
	}

	// now set the label area to the bottom of the slider.
	obj.labelArea = area;
	return obj;
};

laf.registerFunction("getModulatorDragData", singleDragger);

/* now we attach a style sheet to skin the draggers & matrix. */
laf.setInlineStyleSheet("
#controller
{
	background: #222;
	padding: 10px;
	border-radius: 50%;
	border: 1px solid #888;
	gap: 0px;
}

.dragger
{
	background: #999;
	color: #222;
	border-radius: 50%;
	padding-left: 30px;
	padding-right: 10px;
	margin: 10px;
	height: 45px;

	box-shadow: 0px 2px 5px black;
	cursor: grabbing;
}

/* IMPORTANT: this is the pseudo state that is engaged when the dragger is
   the currently selected mod source. */
.dragger:checked 
{
	border: 2px solid white;
	background: #aaa;
}

.dragger::before
{
	content: '';
	background-image: var(--dragPath);
	width: 100vh;
	position: absolute;
	background-color: #555;
	margin: 15px;
}

.dragger:active
{
	transform: scale(95%);
}

/** Make a real minimal matrix with the power of display:none. */
#intensity { display: none; }
#inverted { display: none; }
#plotter { display: none; }
#auxindex { display: none; }
#auxintensity { display: none; }
#mode {	display: none; }
.search { display: none; }

.control-button { display: none; }

");

/** Attach the LAF to the knobs. */
const var Knobs = [ Content.getComponent("Gain"),
					Content.getComponent("Pitch")];

for(k in Knobs)
	k.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);

// And the floating tiles...
Content.getComponent("FloatingTile1").setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
Content.getComponent("FloatingTile2").setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);

